= Photosynthesis — Technical =

== Light-driven phase ==
In oxygenic photosynthesis, light-harvesting systems drive charge separation and electron transport. Water oxidation provides electrons and produces O2. Proton translocation across a photosynthetic membrane can power ATP synthesis, while electron transfer reduces NADP+ to NADPH.

== Carbon fixation ==
The Calvin–Benson cycle uses ATP and NADPH to fix inorganic carbon into organic products. This phase is not simply a photon-absorbing reaction, but it is coupled to the supply of light-reaction products and can be regulated by light conditions.

== Biological diversity ==
Different photosynthetic bacteria may use alternative pigments and electron donors and may perform anoxygenic photosynthesis. A chloroplast-centric explanation is valid for plants and many algae but not universal.

== Evidence and limitations ==
Net biomass accumulation differs from gross carbon fixation because photorespiration, respiration, export, and other metabolic pathways affect the net balance. A photosynthetic electron-transfer pathway should be characterized by organism, illumination, environmental conditions and measurements.

